## Solution

**Notation.** Let $[n]=\{1,2,\dots,n\}$. For an admissible pair $(S,T)$ write $a=|S|$, $b=|T|$.

**Reformulation.** The condition "$s>|T|$ for every $s\in S$" says every element of $S$ exceeds $b=|T|$, i.e.
$$S\subseteq\{b+1,b+2,\dots,n\}.$$
Likewise the condition on $T$ gives
$$T\subseteq\{a+1,a+2,\dots,n\}.$$
Conversely, any pair $(S,T)$ with $|S|=a$, $|T|=b$, $S\subseteq\{b+1,\dots,n\}$, $T\subseteq\{a+1,\dots,n\}$ is admissible. For fixed $a,b$ the two choices are independent, so the number of such pairs is
$$\binom{n-b}{a}\binom{n-a}{b},$$
which is $0$ automatically when $a+b>n$. Hence
$$f(n)\;=\;\sum_{a,b\ge 0}\binom{n-b}{a}\binom{n-a}{b}\;=\;\sum_{a+b\le n}\binom{n-b}{a}\binom{n-a}{b}.$$

**A cleaner single-sum form.** Set $c=n-a-b\ge 0$ (so $a+b+c=n$). Then $n-b=a+c$ and $n-a=b+c$, giving
$$f(n)=\sum_{\substack{a,b,c\ge 0\\a+b+c=n}}\binom{a+c}{c}\binom{b+c}{c}.$$
Fix $c$ and sum over $a+b=n-c$. Using $\binom{a+c}{c}=[x^a](1-x)^{-(c+1)}$ and $\binom{b+c}{c}=[x^b](1-x)^{-(c+1)}$, Vandermonde's convolution gives
$$\sum_{a=0}^{n-c}\binom{a+c}{c}\binom{n-c-a+c}{c}=[x^{\,n-c}](1-x)^{-(2c+2)}=\binom{n+c+1}{2c+1}.$$
Therefore
$$\boxed{\,f(n)=\sum_{c=0}^{n}\binom{n+c+1}{2c+1}\,}.\qquad(\star)$$

**Generating function.** Let $F(x)=\sum_{n\ge0}f(n)x^n$. Put $m=n-c\ge0$ in $(\star)$:
$$F(x)=\sum_{c\ge0}\sum_{m\ge0}\binom{m+2c+1}{2c+1}x^{m+c}
=\sum_{c\ge0}x^c\cdot\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2c+2}}
=\frac{1}{(1-x)^2}\cdot\frac{1}{1-\frac{x}{(1-x)^2}}
=\frac{1}{(1-x)^2-x}
=\frac{1}{1-3x+x^2}.$$
Thus $f$ satisfies $f(n)=3f(n-1)-f(n-2)$ with $f(0)=1$, $f(1)=3$.

**Identification with Fibonacci numbers.** Let $F_k$ be the Fibonacci sequence ($F_1=F_2=1$). The even-indexed Fibonacci numbers satisfy the same recurrence
$$F_{2n+2}=3F_{2n}-F_{2n-2},$$
because $F_{2n+2}=F_{2n+1}+F_{2n}=2F_{2n}+F_{2n-1}=3F_{2n}-F_{2n-2}$. Matching initial values: $f(0)=1=F_2$ and $f(1)=3=F_4$. Hence by induction
$$f(n)=F_{2n+2}\qquad\text{for all }n\ge0.$$

(Equivalently: $\sum_{n\ge0}F_{2n}x^n=\dfrac{x}{1-3x+x^2}$, so $\sum_{n\ge0}F_{2n+2}x^n=\dfrac{1}{1-3x+x^2}=F(x)$.)

**Evaluation at $n=10$.** Using $(\star)$,
$$f(10)=\sum_{c=0}^{10}\binom{11+c}{2c+1}$$
$$=\binom{11}{1}+\binom{12}{3}+\binom{13}{5}+\binom{14}{7}+\binom{15}{9}+\binom{16}{11}+\binom{17}{13}+\binom{18}{15}+\binom{19}{17}+\binom{20}{19}+\binom{21}{21}$$
$$=11+220+1287+3432+5005+4368+2380+816+171+20+1=17711.$$
This equals $F_{22}=17711$ (the Fibonacci values run $F_{20}=6765,\,F_{21}=10946,\,F_{22}=17711$).

**Answer.** The number of admissible ordered pairs of subsets of $\{1,2,\dots,10\}$ is

$$\boxed{17711}.$$

